000100 REM SWITCH / CASE / DEFAULT / ENDSWITCH -- C's fallthrough, undiluted
      *
      * 1. The subject is evaluated once, at SWITCH. Matching is loose ==
      *    (PHP 5, spec 3.3), so this is broader than it looks. Once a CASE
      *    matches, every following CASE and DEFAULT body runs in order,
      *    because there is no `break` -- there never was one to forget.
       $x = 2                                    ;
       SWITCH $x
       CASE 1
       PRINT "one"
       CASE 2
       PRINT "two"
       CASE 3
       PRINT "three"
       DEFAULT
       PRINT "unmatched, in principle"
       ENDSWITCH
      *
      * 2. DEFAULT is not "otherwise" -- it is a label like any other. Put it
      *    last and it behaves like everyone expects; put it first and it
      *    runs first, every time, whether or not anything else would match.
       SWITCH "nope"
       CASE "yep"
       PRINT "2: should not print"
       DEFAULT
       PRINT "2: default only"
       ENDSWITCH
      *
      * 3. BREAK parses (so a program ported from a real language still
      *    loads) and does nothing except say so. Fallthrough is the only
      *    control flow SWITCH has.
       SWITCH 9
       BREAK
       CASE 9
       PRINT "3: matched nine anyway"
       ENDSWITCH
      *
      * 4. Loose == means a numeric-looking string matches the integer it
      *    looks like (PHP 5 table, spec 3.3) -- the same rule that makes
      *    "0" == "a" true elsewhere in this language.
       SWITCH "3"
       CASE 3
       PRINT "4: the string three equals the integer 3 under loose equality"
       ENDSWITCH
       FREE($x)
